       "html": "<p>A <strong>bond</strong> is a loan. The investor lends money to an issuer (a corporation, the U.S. government, or a municipality), and in return the issuer promises two things: to pay <strong>interest</strong> at regular intervals, and to repay the original loan amount on a fixed future date. Because bonds represent debt (not ownership), bondholders are <strong>creditors</strong> of the issuer, not owners. This is the key distinction from stock: a stockholder owns a piece of the company; a bondholder is simply owed money.</p>"
