       "type": "text",
       "html": "<p><strong>Modern Portfolio Theory (MPT)</strong>, developed by Harry Markowitz, holds that investors should evaluate securities not in isolation but by how they affect the <em>whole portfolio's</em> risk and return. By combining assets with low correlation, an investor can build a portfolio that maximizes expected return for a given level of risk. The set of these optimal portfolios is the <strong>efficient frontier</strong>—any portfolio below the frontier is inferior because more return is available for the same risk.</p><p><strong>Asset allocation</strong> is the practical application: dividing the portfolio among asset classes (equities, fixed income, cash, alternatives) to match the investor's objectives, time horizon, and risk tolerance. Studies suggest the asset-allocation decision drives the large majority of long-run portfolio variability—more than individual security selection.</p>"
